sections.s2_technical:
```java
public class Account {
    // INSTANCE method — will be OVERRIDDEN
    public String getDescription() { return "Generic Account"; }

    // STATIC method — will be HIDDEN
    public static String getPolicy() { return "Standard Banking Policy"; }
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    // OVERRIDES parent's instance method:
    @Override
    public String getDescription() { return "Savings Account (4% interest)"; }

    // HIDES parent's static method (NOT @Override — hiding!):
    public static String getPolicy() { return "Savings Special Policy"; }
}
```

The CRITICAL difference — through a PARENT reference:

```java
Account ref = new SavingsAccount("SAV001", BigDecimal.ZERO);

// INSTANCE METHOD — resolved at RUNTIME (actual object = SavingsAccount):
String desc = ref.getDescription();
// → "Savings Account (4% interest)" (Recommended) — JVM dispatches to SavingsAccount!
// RUNTIME: JVM checks actual type in heap → SavingsAccount → calls its version.

// STATIC METHOD — resolved at COMPILE TIME (reference type = Account):
String policy = ref.getPolicy();  // WARNING: accessing static via instance
// → "Standard Banking Policy" ← Account's version! NOT SavingsAccount's!
// COMPILE TIME: compiler sees ref is Account → binds to Account.getPolicy().
// Does NOT check actual object type. NO dynamic dispatch for static.

// PROOF: change the reference type → static result changes:
SavingsAccount saRef = new SavingsAccount("SAV001", BigDecimal.ZERO);
String saPolicy = saRef.getPolicy();
// → "Savings Special Policy" — because reference type IS SavingsAccount now!
// Same object, different reference type → different static method called!
// This proves: static = compile-time binding to REFERENCE TYPE.
```

Why static methods cannot be overridden:

```java
// Static methods belong to the CLASS, not to an INSTANCE.
// They are resolved at compile time using the class name, not the object.
// There is NO vtable entry for static methods — no dynamic dispatch mechanism.

// Account.getPolicy() → resolved at compile time → Account's code
// SavingsAccount.getPolicy() → resolved at compile time → SavingsAccount's code
// No runtime decision happens. No polymorphism.

// @Override on a static method → WARNING or different behavior:
// @Override
// public static String getPolicy() { ... }
// This is NOT an override — it's hiding. The annotation is misleading.
// Some compilers WARN: "static method cannot be overridden"
```

Practical impact — why this matters in production:

```java
public class AccountService {
    public void process(Account account) {
        // INSTANCE: works polymorphically — correct!
        String desc = account.getDescription();  // SavingsAccount → "Savings Account"

        // STATIC: does NOT work polymorphically — BUG if you expect it to!
        String policy = account.getPolicy();  // ALWAYS Account's policy! Even for SavingsAccount!
        // Developer THINKS: "I have a SavingsAccount, so getPolicy() returns savings policy"
        // REALITY: ref type is Account → Account.getPolicy() called. WRONG result!

        // FIX: never call static methods on instance variables.
        // Always use: Account.getPolicy() or SavingsAccount.getPolicy()
        // This makes it OBVIOUS which class's method is called.
    }
}
```

